Ordering repeated incidences in a left partition
================================================

The left missing incidences are processed in nondecreasing leaf order. Several consecutive incidences can belong to the same leaf x. They must go to distinct outside blocks. Their identities carry no mathematical information: the graph specifies only which blocks contain x. Require these copies to choose strictly increasing block indices. A newly created block receives the next index.

For completeness, take any final partition with no repeated leaf in a block. Order its blocks by their least leaf; break ties arbitrarily. Process the leaves in increasing order and, for each leaf, process its blocks in that order. A block whose minimum leaf has already occurred exists when it is chosen. All others are created at that step, in the prescribed order. Thus the new traversal produces exactly this final partition up to block relabelling. Every prefix still satisfies all earlier necessary girth, size and pair tests. The subsequent coupled recursion tries every right attachment to the chosen labelled blocks. Relabelling outside blocks transports any omitted named partition and all its completions to a retained one. This loses no graph and claims no equality of raw partition counts.

Bounded controls compare complete sets of sorted block masks produced by the actual old and new visitors. The earlier traversal and its incomplete target receipt remain preserved; no partial output contributes coverage to the new run.
